 Cries 
 Cries Import a cartridge and press enter. Move with the arrow keys or WASD; doors
 Cries warp between maps and routes run into each other at their edges, with the
 Cries player and NPCs drawn from the cartridge's own
 Cries sprites. Z or space reads a signpost or talks to someone. Walking in tall grass
 Cries starts a battle, where Z confirms and the arrows pick FIGHT, BALL or RUN — a
 Cries caught Pokemon joins your party. Balls come out of the bag and running out of
 Cries them is a real outcome. Item balls lying on the ground are picked up by facing
 Cries them and pressing Z, and stay picked up; so are the 85 items buried in the
 Cries ground, if you press Z on the right square. Talking to a shopkeeper opens their
 Cries counter, stocked with what that particular shop sells; they will buy things back
 Cries at half price, but not your key items. Pick an item and a dial asks how many —
 Cries up and down step by one, left and right by ten, and it will not let you dial
 Cries past what you can pay for or what the bag will hold. X opens the menu — party list, summaries,
 Cries and the bag, whose four pockets are filled from what the cartridge says each
 Cries item is. The Pokédex is there too: every species you have met and every one you
 Cries have owned, and a page on each with its sprite, its classification, its height
 Cries and weight, its typing, its base stats and the two pages of description the
 Cries cartridge itself carries, turned over with left and right. Each map plays its
 Cries own music, synthesised from the cartridge's channel data through a Game Boy
 Cries sound chip written from the hardware specification. F5 saves, and the
 Cries bag, your money and the dex go with it; a save is loaded automatically.
 Cries 
 Cries Drop a cartridge `.sav` onto the window while playing and its party comes
 Cries across. Nothing about the save's layout is hardcoded: the party is found by
 Cries checking that every member's stats come back out of the stat formula.
