 Cries ## Requirements
 Cries 
 Cries - [LÖVE 11.4 or newer](https://love2d.org/)
 Cries - A cartridge image you own, in `.gbc` form
 Cries 
 Cries ## Running
 Cries 
 Cries ```bash
 Cries love .
 Cries ```
 Cries 
 Cries Drag your cartridge image onto the window. The importer reports what it found
 Cries and writes the extracted data under LÖVE's save directory, in `cache/<game>/`.
 Cries 
 Cries There are headless entry points for scripting, since drag-and-drop cannot be
 Cries automated:
 Cries 
 Cries ```bash
 Cries love . --test "C:\path\to\crystal.gbc" report.txt
 Cries ```
 Cries 
 Cries `--import` runs the real pipeline, `--probe-pics` is a format diagnostic. On
 Cries Windows, `scripts\test.ps1 -Rom <path>` wraps the test run and prints the report
 Cries (argument quoting matters — a ROM path with spaces silently truncates otherwise).
 Cries 
 Cries ## How table offsets are found
 Cries 
 Cries Most tools in this space hardcode a table of ROM offsets per game and per
 Cries revision, which fails silently the moment it meets a dump it did not anticipate.
 Cries This project searches instead.
 Cries 
 Cries Every data table begins with known content — species 1 is always Bulbasaur,
 Cries move 1 is always Pound — so the first record can be encoded as a signature and
 Cries searched for. A candidate is only accepted once the entire table decodes without
 Cries a malformed record *and* spot checks on records we did not search for succeed:
 Cries Mew at index 151, Celebi at 251, Struggle's 1 PP. If two offsets both validate,
 Cries the import refuses to guess and says so.
 Cries 
 Cries The upshot is that Gold, Silver, Crystal, and their revisions all work from the
 Cries same code, and a dump that would have decoded into nonsense fails loudly.
 Cries 
 Cries The second half of that is tested rather than asserted. Pass a non-Gen-2
 Cries cartridge as `-Other` and the suite runs every locator against it and requires
 Cries all of them to refuse:
 Cries 
 Cries ```bash
 Cries scripts\test.ps1 -Rom "<crystal>" -Other "<a Gen 1 cartridge>"
 Cries ```
 Cries 
 Cries A Gen 1 image is the interesting adversary, because it pads its species names to
 Cries the same ten bytes in nearly the same encoding — three of the six signatures do
 Cries occur in it, and validation is what throws them out. Finding that test also
 Cries found a real bug: the sprite-palette search used to accept a Pokémon Red image.
 Cries See the architecture notes.
 Cries 
 Cries The **first** half is still only a design claim. Nothing hardcodes Crystal, but
 Cries the importer has only ever been run against Crystal; confirming it against Gold
 Cries or Silver needs a cartridge nobody here has.
 Cries 
 Cries Discovered offsets are recorded in each cache's `manifest.lua`.
 Cries 
